## Founding & History
Grey Sands Vineyard was established by Bob and Rita. Bob completed a Graduate Diploma in Wine at Roseworthy Agricultural College in 1985/86, following three years (1982-1984) spent living in England and traveling through European wine regions, where he observed that the best wines originated from cooler areas with "difficult" soils. The couple purchased the land, which consisted of grassy paddocks, in November 1987. The first rootlings were planted in the vineyard in the winter of 1989. Their initial wines, a Merlot and a Pinot Gris, were bottled under the Grey Sands label in 1998. The vineyard's name, "Grey Sands," was chosen due to the predominant soil type on their property, inspired by John Locke's 1677 description of the soil at Haut-Brion in Bordeaux.

## People
Grey Sands Vineyard is a family-owned and operated winery, with Bob and Rita as the owners. Bob serves as the principal winemaker, while Rita manages other aspects of the operation.

## Vineyard & Site
Grey Sands Vineyard is situated on the western edge of the Tamar Valley in Northern Tasmania. The vineyard is described as a small, family-owned property of 3.5 hectares, though another source indicates 4.5 hectares. The overall property is 14 hectares. The site was specifically chosen for its optimal aspect, slope, and low fertility soil. The predominant soil type is grey sand, with fine silty grey sand extending to approximately 30cm depth at the top of the hill, followed by a hard-pan layer and then clay. At the bottom of the property, clay is closer to the surface.

The vineyard is planted at a high density of 8800 vines per hectare, a practice common in Bordeaux and Burgundy, to leverage the low fertility soils. The farming approach is unirrigated (dry grown), with vines hand-tended, hand-pruned, and fruit hand-picked. This method encourages the vines to develop deep root systems, penetrating through the hard-pan to the moisture-retentive clay beneath.

Grey Sands cultivates a diverse range of grape varieties. Predominantly planted are Pinot Gris, Pinot Noir, Merlot, and Malbec. Other varieties include Pinot Blanc, Chardonnay, Viognier, Malvasia Bianca, Petit Verdot, Shiraz, Aglianico, Touriga Francesca, Cabernet Franc, Lagrein, and Tannat. As of 2025, Carmenere bore fruit for the first time. In 2024, new plantings included Spanish and Portuguese varieties such as Tempranillo, Tinta Cao, Tinta Barroca, Touriga Nacional, and Cinsaut. Currently, 17 different varieties are bearing fruit, from which 11 or 12 distinct wines are made, depending on the season. Another 6 varieties are in the ground, expected to produce fruit in 3-4 years.

## Cellar Door Experience
The cellar door at Grey Sands Vineyard is set within a two-hectare collector's garden that changes with the seasons. Visits and wine tastings are strictly by appointment only, offering an intimate and personalized experience. The cost for a wine tasting is $30 per person. Tastings are conducted in a glass room that offers scenic views. Visitors are welcome to stroll through the gardens. Guests have reported a warm and welcoming experience from Bob and Rita. Wine tasting is restricted to individuals 18 years or older, though younger persons may accompany adults if supervised. A full refund for cancellations is available with 48 hours' notice.

## Food & Hospitality
Tasmanian produce platters are available for visitors, requiring 48 hours' notice. Guests are also welcome to bring their own picnic to enjoy in the gardens. There is no mention of a dedicated restaurant or kitchen beyond these offerings.

## Awards & Recognition
No reliable information found from public sources.

## Sustainability & Certifications
No reliable information found from public sources regarding specific certifications. The vineyard's practices, such as being unirrigated, hand-tended, hand-pruned, and hand-picked, contribute to sustainable viticulture.

## Visiting Practical Info
The address for Grey Sands Vineyard is 6 Kerrisons Road, Glengarry, Tasmania, 7275, Australia. It is located approximately a 30-minute drive north-west of Launceston, covering a distance of 37 kilometres (23 miles). The phone number is 0407 961 167, and the email address is info@greysands.com.au. The official website is www.greysands.com.au. Grey Sands Vineyard maintains a presence on social media with handles such as @greysandsvineyard, grey_sands_vineyard, and greysandsv. The vineyard has partial accessibility for people with special needs, and visitors are encouraged to call to discuss their requirements. The cellar door is closed on Christmas Day.

## Notable / Distinctive
Grey Sands Vineyard distinguishes itself by aiming to produce concentrated, complex, intriguing, and sometimes confronting wines, rather than striving to please every palate. A key aspect of their approach is specializing in intensely flavored wines and undertaking the initial cellaring themselves, releasing wines only when they are ready for enjoyment but still possess significant cellaring potential. This philosophy challenges conventional winemaking. The cellar door experience is unique, set within a two-hectare collector's garden that evolves with the seasons. The vineyard cultivates a diverse array of grape varieties, including many less common in Tasmania, and actively experiments with new plantings, such as Spanish and Portuguese varieties, for future blends. The name "Grey Sands" directly reflects their distinctive soil type and underpins their winemaking philosophy.

